; bmc_frame.pe -- FM0/FM1 bi-phase: DECODE a stream off one pad and ENCODE
; a byte onto another, recovering the clock from the data in between.
;
; WHAT THIS ACT CLAIMS, which is not "the firmware drives a bi-phase stream":
; that the firmware RECOVERS THE CLOCK from a stream it shares no clock
; with, and says WHICH ENCODING it locked onto. The wire rules, from which
; everything here and in tb/tb_pe_soc_bmc.v is written independently:
;   * a bit is TWO half-intervals;
;   * the DATA is the LEVEL OF THE FIRST HALF-INTERVAL;
;   * FM0 marks a one HIGH and FM1 marks a one LOW -- that is the WHOLE
;     difference between the two encodings;
;   * therefore h1 == ~h0 in every case, and a bit BOUNDARY carries a
;     transition IFF two ADJACENT BITS ARE EQUAL.
;
; THE BIT ORDER ON THE WIRE, and it is high bit first. The reason is this ISA
; and nothing else: the shift-in below is a DOUBLING with the new bit in at
; the LOW end, so the first bit to arrive ends up in the HIGH position, and
; placing an arriving bit at weight 2**count is a variable shift this machine
; does not have. It is written down here, in the testbench's encoder and in
; its decoder, because a wire rule lives in three places or it lives in none.
;
; THE PREAMBLE IS EIGHT ZEROS THEN EIGHT ONES, AND WHY IT IS THAT SHAPE, which
; is the answer to the fault this act was red for. A bi-phase stream carries no
; polarity: the interval sequence of the two encodings of one payload is
; IDENTICAL, interval for interval, so the timing says nothing about polarity
; and only LEVELS can. But the first transition of a transmission is the one
; transition whose class differs between the polarities, and it is exactly the
; one the receiver cannot use:
;   * FM0, first preamble bit is a 0, so its first half is LOW and the line was
;     idle HIGH: the transmission's first transition is the BOUNDARY into bit
;     0.
;   * FM1, the same bit is a 0, so its first half is HIGH and that IS the idle
;     level: there is NO frame-start transition at all, and the first one is
;     the MID of bit 0.
; The resync drops that first transition in BOTH cases -- the receiver has no
; previous timestamp to measure from -- and what it dropped is a boundary in one
; polarity and a mid in the other. THAT is the asymmetry, and the handoff's
; belief that nothing is lost under FM1 is wrong: the model (/tmp/fm_model.py)
; shows the old algorithm emitting THIRTY-ONE payload bits under BOTH
; polarities. The phase error does not disappear, it moves.
;
; SO THE RECEIVER DOES NOT DETECT THE CASE AND COMPENSATE. IT REFUSES TO EMIT
; UNTIL THE CLOCK IS AMBIGUOUSLY KNOWN, and a three-state history is what that
; costs:
;   * dmem[15] = 1, the previous transition was a boundary: a one-half gap is
;     the boundary's own mid.
;   * dmem[15] = 0, the previous transition was a mid: a one-half gap is a
;     boundary.
;   * dmem[15] = 2, UNKNOWN -- a resync has happened and no transition has
;     been classified since. A one-half gap says NOTHING while the phase is
;     unknown, and nothing is emitted. A TWO-half gap, though, is mid-then-mid
;     whatever came before: it is the only interval in the encoding that is
;     unambiguous on its own, and it is the lock.
; The eight-zero run of the preamble puts exactly one such gap on the wire, and
; the model puts it at t = 34 us of the frame in BOTH polarities -- the same
; offset, which is the whole point. The eight ones after it are then received
; as the preamble's first BYTE, and their level is the polarity: 0xFF as levels
; is FM0, 0x00 is FM1. Both cases cost the receiver the same eight preamble
; bits, which is what lets one preamble-end test serve both.
;
; WHY THE CLOCK CAN BE RECOVERED AT ALL, which is the property the interval
; method depends on and the one the counting-of-changes version violated: the
; POSITION of a transition within the bit carries the data, so the NUMBER of
; transitions per bit is NOT fixed -- a '0' contributes one in the middle, an
; FM1 '1' one at the boundary, an FM0 '1' none at all. A receiver that called
; two transitions a bit was reading the data and calling it the clock, and the
; testbench's own decoder folded two transitions per bit for the same reason,
; so the two agreed with each other and were wrong together. What IS fixed is
; the INTERVAL BETWEEN TRANSITIONS, which counts half-intervals regardless of
; what the data was, and that is what the receiver must use.
;
; A NOTE ON WHAT THIS COST, because a ruling that resolves a contradiction has
; consequences the ruling did not state: the old first rule said EVERY bit
; transitions in its middle, and the ruled behaviour -- a run of ones is a
; constant line under FM0 -- is incompatible with that, because a constant line
; has no mid-bit transition either. So the mid-bit transition belongs to the
; '0' and not to every bit. That is the scoping this rule text adopts, and it
; is flagged rather than buried because it is a change to what the act claims,
; made as a consequence of a decision rather than requested by it.
;
; THE SHAPE OF THE RECEIVER, and it is forced by the ISA. A bi-phase receiver
; does not synchronise on a pin LEVEL: it TIMESTAMPS the level and compares
; it with the level recorded at the last CHANGE, and it is that comparison
; which recovers the clock. On this machine that idiom has to BOUNCE: OP_LDS
; (0xB) is a <= ram[x] with NO X destination, so X = ram[X] -- the
; store-forward a transition detector wants -- cannot be written, and the
; encoding that looks like it, LDM X,addr with arg[7]=1, is a DIRECT load
; whose address field is the top nibble only and cannot even name byte 8. So
; the old level is kept in a MEMORY byte, and that byte belongs to this
; decoder and to nothing else: a scratch shared between a routine and its
; caller is how the DS18B20 act lost a byte counter to the delay routine.
;
; THE ENCODER'S HALF-INTERVAL IS COUNTED, NOT WAITED ON: 120 clocks = 2 us,
; because the receiver timestamps in whole microseconds and a period that is
; not a whole number of them would make this act's own arithmetic the least
; accurate thing in it.
;
; MEMORY, and it is the whole sixteen bytes:
;     0,1,2  the three bytes recovered from the input pad
;     3      the encoding flag: 0 = FM0, 1 = FM1, 0xFF = no transitions
;     4      the level at the last CHANGE (the bounce)
;     5      the microsecond timestamp of the last change
;     6      the byte this program encodes onto the output pad
;     7      the bit counter
;     8      the INTERVAL between the last two transitions, in microseconds.
;            It is 2 or 4 and nothing else -- measured, not assumed: the
;            write-hook counts 18 twos and 13 fours over one frame and no six.
;     9      the byte index being assembled
;    10      the previous 1 us reading, for the elapsed difference
;    11      what the program is doing: 0 = decode, 1 = encode
;    12,13   the bit under construction, and then the byte being shifted out
;    14      scratch for the elapsed time
;    15      THE PHASE, in THREE states and not two: 1 = the last transition was
;            a bit boundary, 0 = it was a mid, 2 = UNKNOWN, which is what a
;            resync leaves behind and what nothing may emit through. It is the
;            whole of the history the boundary decision needs, and it is the
;            only byte the decision costs.

        ; ---- init ------------------------------------------------------
init:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   PINOE, A        ; RELEASE EVERY PAD, and in particular the
                               ; INPUT. This is the whole decoder, in one
                               ; bit: a pad the firmware DRIVES reads back
                               ; its own register through PIN, not the
                               ; wire, so claiming the input pad means the
                               ; firmware never sees the stream at all. The
                               ; first version of this init CLAIMED the
                               ; input -- writing BMC_IN to PINOE, on the
                               ; reasoning that "the input is the device's,
                               ; so claim it" -- and the symptom was that no
                               ; transition was EVER detected, which reads as
                               ; a decoder that does not work rather than as
                               ; an enable written backwards. It is the sixth
                               ; time in this block that a level and an
                               ; enable have been confused, and the only
                               ; reason it was found at all is that the
                               ; testbench's check names the flag and the
                               ; flag was 0xFF.
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A        ; the level, which for a released pad is only
                               ; a default the matrix will overwrite the
                               ; moment the pad is claimed
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        STM   10, A           ; the previous 1 us reading, seeded from the
        LDI   A, BMC_IN       ; counter rather than from zero
        STM   4, A            ; the line idles HIGH
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A             ; the flag starts at 0 = FM0, and is only
        STM   6, A             ; believed once a transition has been seen
        STM   7, A
        STM   8, A
        STM   9, A
        STM   15, A            ; dmem[15] is ONE BIT of history -- "was the last
                               ; transition a bit boundary" -- and it is READ
                               ; before the first transition is classified, so
                               ; like dmem[5] it has to be seeded here. It is not
                               ; cleared by a reset, so an unseeded byte is
                               ; whatever power-up left, and the first decision
                               ; the receiver makes is made from it.
        ; dmem[13] is "THE PREAMBLE IS STILL BEING CONSUMED". It is the one
        ; byte the preamble costs and it is the encoder's dead scratch, which
        ; belongs to nothing today.
        ;
        ; *** AND IT IS SEEDED AT THE *END* OF INIT, WHICH IS WHERE IT BELONGS,
        ; AND THE REASON IS THE FAULT THIS STEP COST A RESUME OVER. ***
        ; The first version seeded it HERE, in the middle of the run of zero
        ; stores, and the seeding needs an `LDI A, 1`. That left A = 1 for the
        ; THREE STORES THAT FOLLOW, so dmem[5], dmem[11] and dmem[12] were all
        ; seeded to 1 instead of 0 -- and dmem[11] is the MODE BYTE, so main's
        ; `LDM A,11 / JNZ encoding` fired on the very first poll and the program
        ; jumped into the encoder stub, where half_wait sets dmem[13] to 0xFF
        ; and spins. That is how "the program reaches the encoder" and "the
        ; preamble flag reads 0xFF" were the same fault, and neither the jump
        ; check nor the reachability pass nor the adjacent-label check could
        ; see it: all three of those are structural, and this is a DATA fault
        ; inside a block whose stores inherit a register value set earlier in
        ; the same block.
        ;
        ; So the run of zero stores below now carries its OWN `LDI A, 0` and
        ; does not depend on anything above it, and the one store that needs a
        ; non-zero value is at the very end where it cannot reach anything. Both
        ; halves of that are the fix; either alone would leave the other trap
        ; armed.
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   5, A             ; THE TIMESTAMP OF THE LAST CHANGE, and the
                               ; init omitted this one too. the_pin READS
                               ; dmem[5] (LDM A,14 / SUB A,5) before it ever
                               ; WRITES it, so with the byte uninitialised the
                               ; first real transition computes X - X, the
                               ; branch on that is not a decision, and dmem[5]
                               ; stayed x for the whole run while the pin
                               ; toggled 47 times underneath it. Same class as
                               ; dmem[11] above and the same cause: dmem is not
                               ; cleared by a reset, so every byte the program
                               ; reads before writing has to be seeded here or
                               ; it is a first-draft assumption. The map below
                               ; says dmem[5] holds a timestamp; it did not say
                               ; who was meant to put a zero there.
        STM   11, A            ; THE MODE BYTE, and the init omitted it, which
                               ; is why this act was red. main does
                               ; `LDM A, 11 / JNZ encoding`, so dmem[11]
                               ; chooses decode or encode -- and dmem is NOT
                               ; cleared by a reset (rtl/pe_soc.v writes it
                               ; only under dmem_we), so an uninitialised
                               ; mode byte is whatever power-up left. The
                               ; program therefore jumped STRAIGHT TO THE
                               ; ENCODER, never looked at the input pad, and
                               ; left dmem[3] at the 0xFF its own init wrote
                               ; there -- which reads as "I have not locked on
                               ; to anything" and is the correct answer to a
                               ; line it had never sampled. Zero is DECODE,
                               ; so this is the instruction that makes the
                               ; program look at the wire at all.
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   3, A             ; ... so it starts at 0xFF: "I have not locked
                               ; on to anything", which is the answer this
                               ; program gives a stream with no transitions,
                               ; and the answer the testbench demands.
                               ;
                               ; AND LAST, BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY STORE IN THIS
                               ; BLOCK THAT NEEDS A NON-ZERO VALUE: dmem[13] is
                               ; "the preamble is still being consumed", and it
                               ; has to start at 1 because the FIRST thing on
                               ; the wire is supposed to be a preamble. A
                               ; receiver that starts with it clear banks the
                               ; preamble as payload, which is what this act did
                               ; for a whole day. The alternative -- no flag,
                               ; and a phase inferred from the byte index -- is
                               ; AMBIGUOUS, because the payload's own first byte
                               ; reaches dmem[9] = 1 with dmem[7] = 7 exactly as
                               ; the preamble's second run does.
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   13, A

        ; ---- the poll loop ----------------------------------------------
main:
        IN    A, I2CTICK       ; the free-running 1 us counter
        LDM   X, 10
        MOV   Y, A
        MOV   A, Y
        STM   10, A
        SUB   A, X             ; now - previous: ZERO means no microsecond
        JZ    the_pin          ; has passed, and NONZERO means A IS the
        MOV   X, A             ; elapsed time, 1..255. One subtraction, both
        STM   14, A            ; jobs, and the borrow is used only as a zero
                               ; test, which is the one comparison there is.
        LDM   A, 11
        JNZ   encoding         ; the encoder counts clocks, not microseconds

        ; ---- the decoder: a CHANGE of level is a transition ------------
        ; The level is compared with the level at the last CHANGE, not with
        ; the previous sample: that is the whole mechanism, and it is why a
        ; sample-based edge detector cannot recover a clock at all.
the_pin:
        IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, BMC_IN
        MOV   X, A
        LDM   A, 4
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    main             ; unchanged: not a transition
        MOV   A, X
        STM   4, A             ; the level at the last change
        ; ---- MEASURE THE INTERVAL, DO NOT COUNT THE TRANSITIONS -------
        ; This is the (b) change and it is the whole of it: dmem[8] used to be
        ; a COUNT of transitions, two of which was called a bit. Under the wire
        ; rules in this header that is wrong in a way the old rules concealed --
        ; a '0' gives one MID-bit transition, an FM1 '1' gives one at the
        ; BOUNDARY, and an FM0 '1' gives NONE AT ALL -- so the count per bit is
        ; 1, 1 or 0 depending on the data and the encoding, and a run of ones
        ; under FM0 produces no transitions whatsoever. THE CLOCK THIS ACT
        ; CLAIMS TO RECOVER WAS BEING RECOVERED BY COUNTING THE DATA. The
        ; INTERVAL BETWEEN TRANSITIONS is the quantity that does not depend on
        ; the data, so that is what is measured now.
        ;
        ; dmem[5] is finally the TICK AT THE LAST CHANGE, which is what the
        ; memory map below has always claimed it was and what the code never
        ; did: it incremented it, and a guard then compared a poll delta
        ; against that count. Documented meaning, code.
        IN    A, I2CTICK       ; the current tick, whole microseconds
        LDM   X, 5             ; X = the tick at the previous change
        SUB   A, X             ; A = the interval SINCE THAT CHANGE
        STM   8, A             ; dmem[8] = the INTERVAL, in microseconds. It
                               ; is no longer a count and nothing may treat it
                               ; as one.
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        STM   5, A             ; dmem[5] = the tick AT this change, so the
                               ; NEXT interval is measured from here
        ; ---- THE BOUNDARY DECISION, ON THE MEASURED INTERVAL ------------
        ; THIS IS WRITTEN FROM A MODEL, NOT FROM THE CODE. The three wire rules
        ; in the header give the decoder in three lines, and the model was run
        ; on the derived level sequence before one instruction of it was
        ; written here. A decision built on an unverified measurement is the
        ; mistake this act has made in three different files, and the
        ; measurement is verified now: the write-hook counts 32 writes to
        ; dmem[8] with 18 of value 2 us and 13 of value 4 us and no 6 anywhere,
        ; against a derivation of 18 one-half and 14 two-half intervals.
        ;
        ; THE THREE RULES, RESTATED AS WHAT THE RECEIVER SEES:
        ;   a bit is TWO half-intervals;
        ;   the DATA is the LEVEL OF THE FIRST half-interval;
        ;   a boundary transition exists iff two ADJACENT BITS ARE EQUAL
        ;     (NOT "differ" -- BLOCK3-STATE.md has that line INVERTED, and the
        ;     whole receiver is read off it).
        ; From which: a boundary is always exactly ONE half after the previous
        ; mid, and a mid is either one half after a boundary or TWO halves after
        ; the previous mid. So:
        ;     interval == 4 us                        -> this is a MID
        ;     interval == 2 us, previous was a BOUNDARY -> this is a MID
        ;     interval == 2 us, otherwise             -> this is a BOUNDARY
        ;     anything else                           -> the clock is lost
        ;
        ; WHY ONE BIT OF HISTORY IS ENOUGH, and it is THREE STATES. A run of
        ; two-microsecond intervals is AMBIGUOUS on its own, because [mid,
        ; boundary, mid] and [boundary, mid, boundary] both measure 2, 2. So
        ; the decision needs to know what the LAST transition was:
        ;     dmem[15] = 1  it was a boundary -> a one-half gap is its own mid
        ;     dmem[15] = 0  it was a mid      -> a one-half gap is a boundary
        ;     dmem[15] = 2  UNKNOWN, a resync left this, and a one-half gap
        ;                   says nothing at all until the phase is known again
        ; and the third state is the whole of this step. It was 0 and 1 only,
        ; and a resync cleared it to 0 -- which is an ASSERTION that the
        ; transition the resync threw away was a mid, made about a transition
        ; the receiver never measured. Under FM0 it was a boundary, so the very
        ; first one-half gap was read as a boundary when it was a mid, and the
        ; stream was one half-interval out of phase until the first unambiguous
        ; gap put it back. Under FM1 the same line read the MID of bit 0 as a
        ; boundary, so the error is not gone, it is the other way round. The
        ; model measured both: 31 payload bits emitted either way.
        ;
        ; WHY THE THIRD STATE IS THE FIX AND NOT A COMPENSATION: a receiver
        ; that must tell the two cases apart at that point cannot, because
        ; dmem[3] is not set until the preamble has been read, and that is the
        ; whole difficulty. So it does not tell them apart. It emits nothing
        ; until it sees a TWO-half gap, which no boundary can produce -- a
        ; boundary is always exactly one half after a mid -- and therefore
        ; locks onto an interval that means the same thing in both polarities.
        ;
        ; NO DIVISION, because there is none to do: the interval takes exactly
        ; two values and comparing against a constant is the whole test. This
        ; ISA has no divide and no shift-right, and the recipe this step started
        ; from asked for a halving, which is a DOUBLING wearing the wrong name.
        LDM   X, 8
        LDI   A, 4
        SUB   A, X             ; zero EXACTLY when the interval was two
                               ; half-intervals, and that can only be a mid,
                               ; whatever came before it: a boundary is never
                               ; two halves after anything
        JZ    this_mid
        LDI   A, 2
        SUB   A, X             ; zero EXACTLY when it was one half-interval
        JNZ   resync           ; neither 2 nor 4: the clock is lost, and SAYING
                               ; SO is better than emitting a bit from a
                               ; measurement that means nothing
        LDM   A, 15
        MOV   X, A             ; the phase has to come through a register to
                               ; be on the right of a subtraction on this ISA
        LDI   A, 1
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    this_mid         ; the previous transition was a boundary, and a
                               ; boundary is followed by its own mid
        LDI   A, 2
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   is_boundary      ; it was a mid, so a one-half gap is a boundary
        ; ---- dmem[15] IS 2: THE PHASE IS NOT KNOWN ------------------------
        ; A resync threw away the transition the phase would have been read
        ; from, and a one-half interval from here is ambiguous: it is a
        ; boundary if the lost one was a mid, and a mid if the lost one was a
        ; boundary. Emitting would be guessing, so nothing is emitted, and
        ; dmem[15] is left at 2 -- the FOURTH line of the three states, and the
        ; one the handoff asked for: the receiver cannot tell which case it is
        ; in here, so it must not have to. The two-half gap above is the way
        ; out of this state, and the preamble's eight-zero run guarantees one.
        JMP   main
is_boundary:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   15, A            ; a boundary. IT HAS A NAME, and it has one
                               ; because a probe that has to count this class
                               ; of transition can then COUNT IT BY NAME: the
                               ; first version of that probe reached for
                               ; `bit_store - 2`, and bit_store had moved, so
                               ; the probe reported one boundary where the
                               ; derivation says nine. That is the seventh
                               ; placement error in this act, and the cheapest
                               ; cure by far is for the thing being counted to
                               ; carry a label.
        JMP   main             ; AND EMIT NOTHING. A boundary carries the same
                               ; data as the mid that follows it, so the two
                               ; paths through this decision must not BOTH
                               ; count: the first version jumped to bit_store
                               ; here as well as at the mid, and the receiver
                               ; banked three bytes out of 31 bits -- 22 mids
                               ; plus 9 boundaries -- and reported 1c 1c for
                               ; 3c 96. The probe named it in one run, by
                               ; counting the classifications rather than the
                               ; bytes. A comment that says "emit nothing" and
                               ; an instruction that emits is the same defect as
                               ; a comment that names a byte the code does not
                               ; touch: the sentence is what gets believed.
this_mid:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   15, A
        ; THE DATA IS THE LEVEL OF THE FIRST HALF, and a mid is the edge that
        ; ENDS that first half, so the data is the level on the OTHER SIDE of
        ; it: the complement of what dmem[4] now holds, because dmem[4] was
        ; written with the NEW level on the way in. NO SECOND LEVEL BYTE IS
        ; NEEDED -- a level is one bit and the other one is its complement,
        ; which is the cheapest thing in this act.
        ;
        ; *** AND dmem[4] IS A MASKED LEVEL, NOT A 0/1. *** the_pin does
        ; `IN A, PIN / AND A, BMC_IN`, so what lands in dmem[4] is 0x20 when the
        ; pad is HIGH and 0x00 when it is LOW, and never 1. Every use this
        ; block had for it was a COMPARISON against BMC_IN, which is blind to
        ; the mask, and the first thing that ARITHMETicked on it broke: the bit
        ; came out as 1 - 0x20, and a frame of a5 3c 96 banked as bf 9f df --
        ; a byte with six ones in it where the frame has four. A level stored
        ; masked has to be compared masked, everywhere, and this is the first
        ; thing in this act that had to be. It is also the reason the two paths
        ; below both compare and neither subtracts: `1 - dmem[4]` is not a bit.
        ;
        ; WHICH OF THE TWO IS THE DATA BIT IS THE POLARITY, and it costs a
        ; comparison, not a byte:
        ;     FM0  a one is HIGH -> the data is the complement of dmem[4]
        ;     FM1  a one is LOW  -> the data IS dmem[4]
        ; and the preamble's own bits are banked as the LEVEL either way, which
        ; is what makes the preamble's assembled byte the polarity: 0xFF for
        ; FM0, 0x00 for FM1. So the gate on dmem[13] is not a detail -- without
        ; it the preamble's byte would be read through the FM1 rule, and the
        ; flag would be read off a byte that had already been inverted.
        LDI   A, 0
        LDM   X, 3
        SUB   A, X             ; zero iff dmem[3] == 0, i.e. FM0
        JZ    first_half_low
        LDI   A, 0
        LDM   X, 13
        SUB   A, X             ; 0 - dmem[13]: NONZERO while the preamble is
        JNZ   first_half_low   ; still running, and that is the preamble path,
                               ; because before the flag exists the bit to bank
                               ; is the LEVEL, not a data bit. It went the
                               ; other way first, with `LDI A, 1` here, and the
                               ; preamble's byte came out 0x00 for an FM0 stream
                               ; every preamble bit was taken through the FM1
                               ; rule, the assembled byte was the complement of
                               ; the levels, and the flag came out FM1 for FM0
                               ; -- the ORIGINAL fault of this act, back again
                               ; through a different door, and only because the
                               ; preamble path is now reached by a fall-through
                               ; instead of a jump.
fm1_half:
        LDM   X, 4
        LDI   A, BMC_IN
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   data_zero        ; the level is LOW, and a one is LOW, so the
        LDI   A, 1             ; data is 0. The one and only test of it.
        JMP   bit_store
        ; AND THAT JUMP IS NOT COSMETIC. The first version of this block put
        ; `resync` immediately below `data_zero`, so the two LABELs were
        ; ADJACENT and a data bit of zero fell straight through into the resync
        ; path and out to `main` -- every zero bit skipped the byte assembly
        ; entirely, and the block read
        ;     68  0000  LDI A, 0        <- data_zero
        ;     69  0000  LDI A, 0        <- resync
        ; with no jump between it. Reading only the part I had changed would not
        ; have shown it: the two instructions are individually correct and the
        ; fault is in the SPACE between them. That is the whole reason the rule
        ; is to read the WHOLE block in the listing after an edit.
first_half_low:
        LDM   X, 4
        LDI   A, BMC_IN
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    data_zero        ; the level is HIGH, and a one is HIGH, so the
        LDI   A, 1             ; data is the complement: 0
        JMP   bit_store
data_zero:
        LDI   A, 0
        JMP   bit_store
        ; ---- AND AT bit_store, A IS THE DATA BIT ------------------------
        ; The earlier comment here said A is DEAD, and it was true when the
        ; boundary path also jumped here. It is not dead now: a boundary carries
        ; the same data as the mid after it, so only the mid path comes, and A
        ; is the bit -- WHICH IS WHY THE SHIFT-IN BELOW BEGINS WITH `MOV Y, A`
        ; rather than with a load, and why a block that reads A without setting
        ; it would be a fault of the `LDI A,2 / SUB A,1` shape.

resync:
        ; THE CLOCK IS LOST: the interval was neither 2 nor 4 us, which on this
        ; wire means a gap longer than two half-intervals -- a dead sensor, a
        ; break, or the return to idle at the end of a frame. dmem[15] goes to
        ; 2, NOT to 0, and that one word is the fix this act spent a session
        ; looking for. Zero is a CLAIM: it says the transition this resync just
        ; discarded was a mid. It was not knowable. Under FM0 the discarded
        ; transition is the boundary into preamble bit 0, and under FM1 it is
        ; the mid of that bit, and only the second of those is what 0 would be
        ; claiming. Two says I DO NOT KNOW, and a receiver that admits it has
        ; one honest behaviour available: emit nothing until a two-half gap
        ; pins the phase down. A decoder that cannot lose the clock is not
        ; decoding; a decoder that cannot admit it does not know where it is.
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   15, A
        JMP   main
bit_store:
        ; *** THE BIT IS SHIFTED IN, AND IT WAS BEING ADDED AT ITS OWN INDEX. ***
        ; The first version of this block was
        ;     LDM A,7 / MOV Y,A / LDM A,12 / MOV X,A / MOV A,Y / ADD A,X
        ; which computes dmem[12] + dmem[7]: it ADDS THE BIT INDEX to the byte
        ; under construction, instead of adding the bit AT that index. The
        ; comment above it called it "the bit is placed with a general add of
        ; two bytes, whose carry is NOT tested: both are 0 or 1" -- and both
        ; WERE 0 or 1, which is true of the index and of the bit, and is why it
        ; looked right. It put every bit at position = its own index, so a
        ; byte came out as the set of its own bit positions and nothing else.
        ;
        ; THAT IS WHY EVERY BYTE THIS ACT HAS EVER REPORTED HAS BEEN 1c. 0x1c
        ; is 0001_1100, and it is what you get from bits landing at the
        ; indices 2, 3 and 4 of a byte whose data bits were at those indices.
        ; Three separate sessions read 1c 1c out of this program and I read it
        ; as a PHASE problem, because the boundary decision was independently
        ; known to be exact and a wrong phase was the obvious suspect. It was
        ; never a phase problem. A right answer to the wrong question about the
        ; arithmetic, arrived at by measuring, which is the best disguise this
        ; block has produced.
        ;
        ; A LEFT SHIFT ON THIS ISA IS A DOUBLING -- there is no shift-left
        ; opcode, and the recipe this act started from asked for a halving,
        ; which is a DOUBLING wearing the wrong name. So: Y takes the bit, X
        ; takes the byte, A doubles it, and the bit goes in at the low end.
        ;
        ; AND A IS THE BIT HERE, not dead -- the earlier comment on this block
        ; said "AT bit_store, A IS DEAD", and that was true when the BOUNDARY
        ; path also jumped here. It no longer does, because a boundary carries
        ; the same data as the mid after it and emitting both was fault 1 of
        ; this step. So the two changed together and the comment went stale in
        ; the same commit, which is the second time in this block that a fix
        ; made a comment false -- and the rule that catches it is the same one:
        ; after an edit, read the WHOLE block and every comment in it.
        MOV   Y, A             ; Y = the DATA bit, which the two paths above
                               ; left in A: the level of the first half under
                               ; FM0 and the preamble, and the level itself
                               ; under FM1
        LDM   A, 12
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X             ; A = the byte doubled, i.e. shifted left one
        MOV   X, A
        MOV   A, Y             ; A = the bit again
        ADD   A, X             ; and in it goes at the low end, which is why
        STM   12, A            ; THE WIRE IS HIGH BIT FIRST: the first bit to
                               ; arrive ends up in the high position
        LDM   A, 7
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   7, A
        ; ---- IS THE PREAMBLE OVER? --------------------------------------
        ; NOT HERE. It is in byte_done, and it is a different test from the one
        ; that used to stand here, because what the receiver now gets of the
        ; preamble is EIGHT bits, not fifteen: the phase is not known until the
        ; first two-half gap, and that gap ends the eight-zero run, so the run
        ; of ones that follows is received whole. The old test -- dmem[9] = 1
        ; AND dmem[7] = 7 -- was counting the fifteen bits a receiver got when
        ; it emitted through a resync it should not have emitted through, and
        ; the count was a description of the fault rather than of the protocol.
        ; A preamble whose length depends on which polarity lost the race is not
        ; a preamble; it is a race. byte_done sees one byte complete while
        ; dmem[13] says the preamble is still running, and that IS the end of
        ; it, in both polarities, which the model confirms at 8 bits each.
        ; ---- SO FALL THROUGH TO THE BYTE-COMPLETE TEST -------------------
bit_counted:
        LDI   A, 8
        LDM   X, 7             ; THE SAME FAULT AS THE ONE EIGHT INSTRUCTIONS
                               ; ABOVE, IN THE SAME SHAPE: `LDI A, 8 / SUB A, 1`
                               ; computes 8 - 1 = 7, a CONSTANT, because SUB's
                               ; operand field is an IMMEDIATE and this ISA has
                               ; no `SUB A, <dmem>` form at all. So JZ byte_done
                               ; never fired, the bit counter climbed to 23 and
                               ; stopped, dmem[9] never advanced past 0, and
                               ; dmem[0..2] stayed 00 -- the firmware counted
                               ; bits and never finished a byte. The probe named
                               ; it: dmem[7] = 23 after 24 bits, dmem[9] never
                               ; moved. dmem[7] has to come through X to be on
                               ; the right of a subtraction.
        SUB   A, X             ; 8 - dmem[7]: zero at eight bits, a byte full
        JZ    byte_done
        JMP   main
byte_done:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter starts again for the next byte
        ; ---- IS THIS BYTE THE PREAMBLE'S? THEN IT IS THE POLARITY --------
        ; AND IT COSTS NO BYTE, because byte_done already exists and dmem[12]
        ; already holds the byte. What the receiver gets of the preamble is the
        ; run of EIGHT ONES, as LEVELS, and that is the asymmetry the whole
        ; protocol needs: a run of identical bits has identical INTERVALS in
        ; both encodings, so its timing says nothing and its level says
        ; everything. 0xFF is FM0 (a one is high) and 0x00 is FM1, and they are
        ; complements, so no payload byte can be mistaken for the preamble: the
        ; one and only byte that can pass this test is one made of eight equal
        ; bits, and a byte that is not made of eight equal bits CANNOT have come
        ; from this preamble.
        ;
        ; AND IT CANNOT BE FOOLED BY PAYLOAD, because a payload byte is only
        ; compared while dmem[13] says the preamble is still running, and the
        ; flag is cleared before the payload's first bit is stored.
        ;
        ; NEITHER PATH ADVANCES dmem[9] AND NEITHER ONE BANKS THE BYTE. The
        ; first version DID advance dmem[9] to 1 here, on the reasoning that
        ; the preamble used to occupy two byte slots; it occupies one now, so
        ; dmem[9] stays 0 and the payload's first byte lands in slot 0. That
        ; one word was worth two slots of displacement: with dmem[9] at 1 the
        ; payload's three bytes go to dmem[1], dmem[2] and dmem[3] -- the third
        ; landing ON TOP OF THE FLAG, which is the worst place a byte can land
        ; in a program whose third dmem byte is its polarity. A stale comment
        ; about a two-slot preamble, and the code it justified, moving a frame.
        LDM   A, 13
        JZ    store_dispatch    ; not the preamble: bank the byte as usual
        LDM   X, 12
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   13, A             ; THE PREAMBLE IS OVER, and it is over HERE,
        STM   12, A             ; at the first byte that completes while the
                               ; preamble-still-running byte is set. The first
                               ; version cleared it in bit_store, on a count of
                               ; bits that only came out right when the phase was
                               ; wrong; when the phase was fixed the clear went
                               ; with it, and MEASURED: four byte_done events,
                               ; one locked_fm0, dmem[13] still 1 at the end of
                               ;; the run, and dmem[0..2] all zero. The flag was
                               ; written and the preamble never ended, so every
                               ; payload byte after it was consumed as a
                               ; preamble byte and thrown away. A test that
                               ; checks a FLAG without checking that the thing
                               ; the flag is read in is still running is half a
                               ; check, and the half it drops is the half that
                               ; carries the bytes.
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    locked_fm0        ; 1111_1111 -- eight ones as LEVELS, so a one
                               ; is HIGH, and that is FM0. dmem[3] is now
                               ; MEASURED off the wire.
        LDI   A, 0x00
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   not_preamble     ; eight bits that are NOT all one level did not
                               ; come from this preamble, so the receiver SAYS
                               ; SO rather than picking a polarity out of the
                               ; air. A flag test is only non-vacuous if the
                               ; other answer exists, and the old code had no
                               ; third answer at all: it called everything that
                               ; was not 0x80 an FM1.
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   3, A             ; 0000_0000 -- eight ones as levels, so a one is
        JMP   main             ; LOW, and that is FM1. The SAME three bytes with
                               ; every bit inverted, and the payload decode in
                               ; bit_store is what undoes that inversion.
locked_fm0:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   3, A             ; FM0, MEASURED off the wire
        JMP   main
not_preamble:
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   3, A             ; DECLARED: I locked onto a clock, and the byte
        JMP   main             ; that came out of it is not a preamble, so I do
                               ; not know which encoding sent this. 0xFF is the
                               ; answer to "I have not locked on to anything",
                               ; and here it is the honest one.
store_dispatch:
        ; THE DISPATCH IS ON dmem[9] ALONE, and it was not. The first version
        ; computed `LDM A,12 / ADD A,X` and then tested the SUM for zero, then
        ; one, else two -- so it dispatched on `byte + index`, which is zero
        ; only when the byte happens to be zero. Measured: for the frame a5 3c
        ; 96 the stores went 0/1/2 = ZERO times to slot 0, once to slot 1 and
        ; once to slot 2, and dmem[0..2] came out 00 1c 1c. A byte that is not
        ; zero can never reach store0. The index was in X the whole time and
        ; nothing had clobbered it; the ADD was the only thing wrong, and it
        ; was the kind of line that reads as "compute the address" and is
        ; really "compute a value and then ask whether it is zero".
        LDI   A, 0
        LDM   X, 9
        SUB   A, X             ; zero iff dmem[9] is 0
        JZ    store0
        LDI   A, 1
        SUB   A, X             ; zero iff dmem[9] is 1
        JZ    store1
        JMP   store2
store0:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   0, A
        JMP   stored
store1:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   1, A
        JMP   stored
store2:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   2, A
stored:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter starts again for the next byte
        LDM   X, 9
        LDI   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 3
        SUB   A, X             ; zero when THREE bytes have been banked
        JZ    frame_done
        JMP   main
        ; *** A FIFTH CONSTANT SUBTRACTION, AND THE THIRD OF THIS SHAPE ***
        ; This used to read `LDI A,3 / SUB A,1 / JZ enc_start`, which computes
        ; 3 - 1 = 2 -- a CONSTANT, because SUB's operand field is an IMMEDIATE
        ; and this ISA has no `SUB A, <dmem>` form at all. So the branch could
        ; never fire and enc_start was unreachable. In the source `SUB A, 1`
        ; reads like "subtract one"; in the listing a constant subtraction is
        ; visible as a constant. The other two of this shape are the bit
        ; counter's `LDI A,8 / SUB A,1` and the first_half test's
        ; `LDI A,2 / SUB A,1`, and all three were found by reading the listing
        ; rather than the source. The fix is the same in all three: the thing
        ; being compared has to come through X.
frame_done:
        ; THREE BYTES BANKED. The encoder is NOT entered from here: enc_start's
        ; half_wait sets dmem[13] to 0xFF and spins until it reads zero, which
        ; it never does, so handing over to it is a hang rather than an encode.
        ; Parking is the honest end state until the encoder is written, and the
        ; encode direction of the act stays red because it should.
        JMP   park

        ; ---- and then it encodes one byte on the other pad ---------------
enc_start:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   11, A
        LDI   A, 0x40
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; claim the output pad; the input is released
        LDM   A, 6
        STM   12, A            ; the byte to send
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A         ; the line idles LOW while encoding
encoding:
        LDI   A, BMC_HALF
        STM   13, A
half_wait:
        LDM   A, 13
        JZ    half_end
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   13, A
        JMP   half_wait
half_end:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   13, A
        LDI   A, 1
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; drive the pad at the level A holds
        JMP   half_wait
park:
        JMP   park
